      "latency_ms": 46224.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1197,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound with the chemical formula **H₂O**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more electronegative (it pulls electrons more strongly) than hydrogen, the electrons in the bond are pulled toward the oxygen. This creates a \"bent\" shape where the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. The Only Common Substance in All Three States\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a **solid, liquid, and gas** under standard temperature and pressure conditions.\n*   **Solid:** Ice (frozen water)\n*   **Liquid:** The water we drink and bathe in\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor (invisible gas in the air)\n\n### 3. Key Physical Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients and waste in living organisms.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This is why water forms droplets and allows small insects to walk across ponds.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most substances, water is less dense as a solid (ice) than as a liquid. This is why ice floats on water, insulating lakes and oceans and allowing life to survive underneath the ice.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**. It is found in oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, underground aquifers, and even in the atmosphere as humidity.\n\n### 5. Biological Importance\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. It is the primary component of all living cells (about 60% of the human body is water) and is necessary for:\n*   Chemical reactions in the body.\n*   Temperature regulation (sweating and panting).\n*   Lubricating joints and organs."
